# Example .env file
# Copy this to .env and set your custom password
# Then run with: docker-compose up -d

# RabbitMQ password - will be used in both RabbitMQ and config.json
RABBITMQ_PASSWORD=your-secure-password-here 

# Let's Encrypt email address for certificate registration and renewal notifications
ACME_EMAIL=your_real_email@example.com

# Let's Encrypt domain for which to obtain the SSL certificate
API_DOMAIN=api.yourdomain.com

# --- Ports of the server (optional) ---
# The ports Traefik listens on, as a number. 80 and 443 when not set.
# Only change them if another program needs those ports, and something in
# front (a router, a firewall, another proxy) forwards 80 and 443 to these:
# TradingView sends webhooks to 80 and 443 only, and Let's Encrypt checks
# the domain on 80. The install script opens these in the firewall.
# HTTP_PORT=80
# HTTPS_PORT=443
# The admin interface's second HTTPS port is ADMIN_PORT, below.

# --- Admin web interface (optional) ---
# Uncomment the three lines to start it. It is served on its own domain, which
# must point to this server, and only to the addresses listed here.
# Set its password with `wataadminpasswd` after starting the application.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=admin
# ADMIN_DOMAIN=admin.yourdomain.com
# Comma-separated, your own addresses only (add /32 to the end of single IPs).
# From a phone, whose address changes: the range of a VPN (see the README).
# ADMIN_ALLOWED_IPS=203.0.113.10/32
#
# Without a domain for it: give it the API domain on a second HTTPS port,
# https://<API_DOMAIN>:8443/. All three lines, with COMPOSE_PROFILES and
# ADMIN_ALLOWED_IPS above. 0.0.0.0 publishes the port over IPv4 only, where
# Traefik sees the caller's address.
# ADMIN_DOMAIN=api.yourdomain.com
# ADMIN_ENTRYPOINT=adminsecure
# ADMIN_PORT=0.0.0.0:8443

# --- Price recorder (optional) ---
# Keeps ten-second prices of the turbos and their index for backtesting.
# Add "recorder" to the profiles (COMPOSE_PROFILES=admin,recorder for both)
# and set trade.config.price_recorder.enabled to true in config.json.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=recorder

# --- An alert that does not need this server (optional) ---
# The address of an outside watcher (a dead-man's switch: healthchecks.io, Cronitor,
# Better Stack... any that alerts by itself when it is no longer pinged). The position
# monitor pings it every minute while it hears from Saxo's stream: when the server,
# its network or its RabbitMQ is down, Telegram says nothing, the watcher does.
# Only the position monitor's container receives it; it is never written in a log.
# WATA_HEARTBEAT_URL=

# --- Capital.com feed (optional) ---
# The Nasdaq 100 from Capital.com, live and recorded (what TradingView's
# CAPITALCOM chart shows). Add "capital" to the profiles (with the admin and the
# recorder: COMPOSE_PROFILES=admin,recorder,capital), switch the feed on from the
# admin's Data > Providers page (trade.config.capital_feed.enabled), and give
# its login here. The key is made in Capital.com's Settings > API integrations
# (two-factor authentication must be on); the password is the one chosen for the
# key, not the account's. Only the feed's container receives these three lines.
# Put a value in single quotes if it contains a $ sign.
# CAPITAL_API_KEY=
# CAPITAL_IDENTIFIER=you@example.com
# CAPITAL_PASSWORD=

# --- TA engine (optional) ---
# WATA's own signals: the signal rules of the admin's Technical Analysis pages,
# whose Pine scripts run on the bars of the Capital.com feed. Add "ta" to the
# profiles (with the admin and the feed: COMPOSE_PROFILES=admin,capital,ta) and
# set trade.config.ta_engine.enabled to true in config.json. A rule only
# journals its signals until it is switched to live in the admin.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=ta
